## Certify size from separation

A window whose points all satisfy $|z|\ge\delta>0$ obeys $|H(Z)|\le |Z|/\delta$.

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the counting-transfer theorem</summary>
The triangle inequality gives $|\sum z^{-1}|\le\sum |z|^{-1}$. Each term is at most $1/\delta$. Thus a point-count bound $|Z_n|\le B(n)$ implies $|H(Z_n)|\le B(n)/\delta$. In particular, a count of shape $C\log n/\log\log n$ transfers to the same harmonic shape, multiplied by $1/\delta$.
</details>

## Exact musical ratios from quadratic data

The cleanest exact interval mechanism comes from a quadratic factor. If roots have sum $l$ and nonzero product $q$, their reciprocal harmonic is $l/q$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the reciprocal-root proof</summary>
For distinct nonzero roots $\alpha$ and $\beta$, $1/\alpha+1/\beta=(\alpha+\beta)/(\alpha\beta)=l/q$. Rational $l$ and $q$ therefore give an exact rational harmonic. This is the same symmetric-root principle described by [Viète's formulas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vieta%27s_formulas).
</details>

## The indispensable empty-window check

If a cutoff contains no points, its harmonic is the empty sum, hence zero. Any octave, fifth, or transcendence interpretation must first establish that the relevant window is nonempty. This keeps spectral storytelling tied to the actual mathematics.
